Virtual mallocng opens a meta-area reservation with one mprotect

Without brk (patch 0009), mallocng takes meta areas from an anonymous
reservation mapped PROT_NONE, n = 2, 4, 8, ... pages behind a guard page,
and makes them readable and writable one page at a time as metas are
needed. Upstream a page holds dozens of metas; here a meta carries its
slots' capabilities (cap_slots, about 3 KiB for 32 slots), so nearly every
new group takes a page, and every mprotect is a round trip to the
launcher: 268 of them in the SPEC CPU2017 gcc test job. The virtual
profile now opens all of a reservation but its guard page in one call,
before the reservation is published (a failed call unmaps it, so the next
allocation retries rather than writing into PROT_NONE memory); pages not
yet used still cost nothing until first touched.

--- a/src/malloc/mallocng/malloc.c
+++ b/src/malloc/mallocng/malloc.c
@@ -82,10 +82,25 @@
 			p = mmap(0, n*pagesize, PROT_NONE,
 				MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON, -1, 0);
 			if (p==MAP_FAILED) return 0;
+#ifdef CAPSTONE_MUSL_MALLOC
+			// A meta here carries its slots' capabilities, about a page each,
+			// and every mprotect is a request to the launcher. Open all but
+			// the guard page at once, before the reservation is published;
+			// untouched pages still cost nothing.
+			if (mprotect(p + pagesize, (n-1)*pagesize, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE)
+			    && errno != ENOSYS) {
+				munmap(p, n*pagesize);
+				return 0;
+			}
+#endif
 			ctx.avail_meta_areas = p + pagesize;
 			ctx.avail_meta_area_count = (n-1)*(pagesize>>12);
 			ctx.meta_alloc_shift++;
 		}
+#ifdef CAPSTONE_MUSL_MALLOC
+		// Without brk every meta area comes from a reservation opened above.
+		need_unprotect = 0;
+#endif
 		p = ctx.avail_meta_areas;
 		if ((uintptr_t)p & (pagesize-1)) need_unprotect = 0;
 		if (need_unprotect)
